Call GCC's math builtins from the three constexpr functions that need them.

<cmath> is only half constexpr.  libstdc++ writes the float and long double
overloads itself, as one-line wrappers over __builtin_floorf and friends, and
those are constexpr; the DOUBLE overload is `using ::floor;', the C library's
function, and a C declaration cannot be constexpr.  So a constexpr function
that calls std::floor(double) contains a call to a non-constexpr function.

C++23 made that legal as long as the call is never constant-evaluated (P2448R2,
"Relaxing some constexpr restrictions"), and upstream relies on it -- the
FIXME above WTF's fabsConstExpr says as much: "Replace with std::fabs() once
std::fabs() is constexpr.  requires C++23".  GCC implements P2448R2 from 13
onwards.  Built by a g++ 12, which is the newest Ubuntu 22.04 packages and is
the version WebKit itself asks for (WebKitCompilerFlags.cmake wants >= 12.0.0),
the same source stops at

    Source/WTF/wtf/Seconds.h:216:36: error: call to non-'constexpr'
    function 'double floor(double)'

four objects into WTF.  Upstream never sees it because the diagnostic depends
on the call NOT being foldable, and -fno-builtin is what stops the fold: with
builtins on, gcc recognises floor() as its own and folds it, so the constexpr
body is fine on any version.  This port compiles with -fno-builtin (see
toolchain/likeos-cc), so the fold is off and the compiler is left with the
declaration.

Naming the builtin directly is what both halves of that sentence agree on:
-fno-builtin only stops gcc from recognising a LIBRARY name as a builtin, so an
explicit __builtin_floor still folds, and it is exactly what libstdc++'s own
constexpr overloads call one line away.

THE VALUES DO NOT CHANGE, which is the only thing that could reach rendering:

  floor    is an exact operation -- the result is representable, so there is
           nothing to round and no accuracy to lose.  gcc expands
           __builtin_floor inline (cvttsd2si, then a fixup for the negative
           case and for -0.0) instead of calling the libc, and the two agree
           on every input, NaN and infinity included: those take the
           |x| >= 2^52 arm and come back unchanged.  Cheaper, too -- the call
           is gone -- so nothing here can get slower.
  fmod     is not expanded inline at all.  Both spellings compile to the same
           call to the same function; verified by diffing the assembly, which
           is identical but for a label name.

A fourth candidate was left alone deliberately.  WebCore's computeDeltaEOK
(ColorConversion.h) calls the THREE-argument std::hypot, which is not
constexpr either -- but libstdc++ implements that one itself, by scaling on
the largest component, and hypot(hypot(a, b), c) is a different computation
that disagrees with it in the last bit of the float for about 40% of inputs
(measured over 6.2M sample points).  It also needs no fix: the function is a
template with no caller anywhere in the tree, so it is never instantiated and
the diagnostic never fires.  Colour difference is exactly where a last-bit
difference could change a gamut-mapping step, so it keeps upstream's arithmetic
untouched.

The fifth, WTF's isIntegral(float), is fine as it stands -- std::isinf(float)
and std::trunc(float) are the overloads libstdc++ writes, not the ones it
inherits from the C library.

These are every such site in the tree: found by walking each constexpr function
body in Source/ (ThirdParty included) for a std:: math call, then compiling
each candidate with the port's own flags.

--- a/Source/WTF/wtf/Seconds.h
+++ b/Source/WTF/wtf/Seconds.h
@@ -213,7 +213,9 @@
 
     constexpr Seconds reduceTimeResolution(Seconds resolution)
     {
-        double reduced = std::floor(seconds() / resolution.seconds()) * resolution.seconds();
+        // __builtin_floor, not std::floor: libstdc++ leaves the double
+        // overload as the C library's floor(), which is not constexpr.
+        double reduced = __builtin_floor(seconds() / resolution.seconds()) * resolution.seconds();
         return Seconds(reduced);
     }
 

